{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "Square short pulse: 2D RZ small emitter, Bz = 100 T",
  "description": "Short square electron pulse from a small circular emitter in the Heri-Zhang (2025) diode: gap d = 1.5 mm, V0 = 30 kV, axisymmetric R-Z, electrostatic, axial magnetic field Bz = 100 T. Emitter radius R/d = 0.02944 (requested 0.03; R = 44.16 um after grid alignment); outer radial boundary 4.8 mm = 3.2 d (dphi/dr = 0, absorbing). T_CL = 3 d / v*, v* = sqrt(2 e V0 / m) (43.81 ps); square pulse of duration k_p T_CL = 0.1 T_CL (4.381 ps) starting at t = 0, cold emission (energy 0), charge per area Q/A = f eps0 V0 / d with f = 1 (J = 4.04e7 A/m^2); observation continues 5 T_CL after the pulse (t_end = k_p T_CL + 5 T_CL = 223.4 ps). Bz = 100 T confines the flow: species motion = z_only (electrons move only along +z, the infinite-B limit; the gyro-radius at 100 T is far below one cell). motion = full uses the 100 T field but then dt must resolve the cyclotron period (0.36 ps). Mesh: fine and uniform along the beam column r <= R from cathode to anode (dz = d/256, dr = R/10), growing smoothly to a uniform radial spacing above the emitter. dt = pulse/200 (21.9 fs), so the pulse spans 200 steps. Select Emission > pulsed_beam > Current pulse to change the pulse, and Velocity distribution to change the emitted normal-speed flux; click Apply, then Run. Old results are cleared when model settings change. Illustrative source/transport example, not a published benchmark.",
